**Objective**

This paper presents:  
•	A background of past work on data quality assurance processes.
•	Draft protocols for EM data quality assurance processes to guide national processes.
•	Draft protocols for the verification of catch for target species, bycatch and SSIs.  
•	Proposed next steps in the development of EM Data Quality Assurance processes. 
 
**1.	Background**

Data Quality Assurance (DQA)
The aim of data quality assurance processes or debriefing with respect to at sea observers is to establish the truth of the data produced by an observer through verification and validation processes. 
Under the PIRFO training framework data quality assurance processes are guided by the core principles of: 
•	Validity 
•	Transparency 
•	Fairness 
•	Consistency 
•	Timeliness 
With respect to e-monitoring the debriefer is referred to as the EM data quality reviewer or reviewer hereafter in this document. 

Data quality assurance in E-Monitoring 
Over the past decade of EM trials, national EM programme development and progress of regional standards and guidelines, data quality assurance has been a focus in various discussions and work streams at the national and regional level. 

2016: During E-Monitoring trials in Fiji a standard operating procedure for data quality was developed specifically for use with Satlink SVM EM-review software version at the time. The SOP outlined checks for a reviewer in different stages of EM analysis including setting, pre-hauling and hauling. This highlights that exact protocols may be system specific. 

2017: Debriefing in the context of E-Monitoring was a central focus of the second EM process standard workshop held in Noumea, New Caledonia, which brought together participants from 13 Member countries and four EM service providers. The workshop discussed key elements of EM debriefing, including the need for regional standards, terminology clarity, second-person reviews, independent oversight, and data validation approaches. The group recommended the development of regional EM debriefing standards and the integration of EM training, including debriefing, into the PIRFO framework. Participants also noted the strength of EM in providing a permanent record for review and discussed the need for feedback form the reviewer. 

2022: The FFA EM Standards Specifications and Procedures provide a guide to EM data quality assurance processes under SSP 2b (listed below). The SSPs present a sequential order of workflow from EM analysis to verification and validation processes. 

a.	EM Data Quality Reviewer will conduct the systematic quality assurance review by re-analysing a stipulated proportion of the EM Data generated by the EM Analyst, including consideration of coverage and (field) completeness. 

b.	Verification includes a review of a subset of the Minimum EM Data fields as determined by the EM program such as, but not limited to: 
i.	 Vessel identification 
ii.	Trip information (i.e., vessel plan) 
iii.	 Set and haul information 
iv.	 Gear information 
v.	Timestamp accuracy 
vi.	 Geolocation accuracy (within 100 metres) 
vii.	 Species identification 
viii.	 Length frequency measurements 
ix.	 Fate determination 
x.	 IUU events 
xi.	 Compliance violations 
xii.	 DRC and reviewer information 

c.	 EM review output/report should indicate review protocol, including whether all fields or a specific subset of fields were reviewed, and whether a general compliance review has been conducted. 

d.	 EM reviews will be conducted according to agreed protocols. 
 
The purpose of validation is to ensure that EM Data is consistent with other trip data such as VMS, eLogs, and onboard observer reports. Validation should be performed on EM Data using the following analogous, independent sources: 

a.	 VMS will be used to validate time and position information. 
b.	 Where available, eLogs and observer reports will be used to validate EM Data such as catch and species information as well as violations or compliance issues. 
c.	VMS (and eLogs, where available) will be used to compare vessel and trip information. 
d.	 Unloading, transhipment, boarding, and port inspection reports to verify total retained trip catch by species. 
e.	 Automated AI analysis, where applicable. 

 2023: DCC strategic plan 2023-2027 draws from prior discussions and highlights that: 

a) Determine EM Data Quality Review (verification) protocols for re-analysing a stipulated proportion of the EM Data generated by the EM Analyst, including  
consideration of coverage and (field) completeness. The DCC will specify the  
protocols and coverage EM Data Quality Reviews.  

b) EM Data Quality Validation protocols to ensure that EM Data is consistent with other independent trip data comparisons against VMS, eLogs, and onboard observer reports.  

c) EM data quality reviews and audits require the standardised data QA processes. 
 
2024: The DCC Longline E-Monitoring Minimum Data Fields Standard was approved by FFC. The fields had been developed through earlier work with consideration of data quality assurance. Field descriptions and protocols for the EM analyst described in the standard provide a basis for defining reviewer protocols. 

2025: To support DQA processes, the DCC Longline EM Minimum Data Fields Standard has been made available in a structured JSON format. This machine-readable version, introduced at DCC12 in 2022 and hosted on GitHub (updated in June 2025), facilitates transparent version control, continuous stakeholder input and practical implementation. It complements the broader adoption of EM standards by both FFA and WCPFC in 2024 and serves as a foundational tool to improve consistency and data quality across national EM programs. 

Building on the background and prior work, the next section outlines draft protocols for EM data quality control to support national EM programme and regional standards development.

**2. Draft protocol for EM data quality assurance process**

The draft protocol is provided as guidance for national EM programs and draw from field descriptions and protocols as agreed in the DCC Longline EM Minimum Data Field Standard. 
 
Three additional columns are added to the DCC Longline E-Monitoring Minimum Data Fields table (Appendix 1): 
•	Review Priority
•	Verification Protocol 
•	Validation Protocol 

2.1 Assigning priorities for review 

Fields were assigned a High, Medium, or Low review priority based on:
•	how the data is generated, 
•	whether the field is mandatory or non-mandatory,
•	 and its relevance to catch, effort, and potential compliance information. 

Reviewers can prioritise fields for closer scrutiny based on assigned priority level.

•	High = Mandatory and entered by Analyst 
•	Medium= Non-Mandatory but required to provide information about catch, effort or incident, or mandatory but may not be encountered (Special gear attributes fields) 
•	Low= Auto generated by the EM system or linked to electronic registries where there is less likely to be errors. 

The review priorities are initial suggestions and will be adjusted based on feedback from national programs through experience with EM systems. 


2.2 Description of validation and verification protocols and processes

The descriptions in the columns for Verification Protocol and Validation Protocol were drafted considering field descriptions and protocols for EM analysts.

It should be noted that the data field descriptions and protocols for EM analysts for EM were developed based on experience with only the Satlink EM analysis software version at the time. Knowledge and experience of other EM software and hardware by Members and input by EM Service providers will be considered in the review of the fields, descriptions, EM analyst protocols, automated DQA within EM systems and use of AI which may impact the data quality assurance protocols in future. 

Verification (Human) process

After the EM analyst completes the analysis of the trip. The reviewer should be able access to the analysis software and the EM trip summary report where checks will be conducted.

In verifying EM data, depending on the type of field the reviewer will be checking for
•	Completeness of fields and formats
•	Logical consistency
•	Compliance with protocol
•	Review of unusual or critical events
•	Independent re-analysis 

Reviewers will produce a report for feedback to the analyst and for program management purposes a format and point of access for which is yet to be developed and determined.

Validation process (automated)
Once verification by the reviewer is complete the trip is sent to Tufman2 or other repositories designated by national programs.
Within Tufman2 EM data can be cross-checked with
•	Observer data
•	Logsheets/elogs
•	Unloadings
•	Port Sampling
•	VMS
Errors or discrepancies found may trigger other actions including specific review of EM video footage.

2.3 Draft review protocol for verification of catch  

The reviewer will verify the species identification assigned by the analyst to catch.
A key question is how much catch verification is required for target species, bycatch and SSI’s respectively?

A scientific and cost benefit reasoning for the proportion of catch data that should be verified by the reviewer is yet to be conducted. However, it is recommended that  
EM analysis coverage rates for target species should be 20% of sets per trip to have sufficient precision in scientific analysis.

Therefore, until there is specific advice on the level of catch verification by the reviewer the suggested coverage for review will be all catch in 20% of sets as a baseline, inclusive of bycatch and SSI’s which are rarer in occurrence.

Where analysis coverage rates vary, reviewers should maintain verification of all catch identification.
 
This will have cost and efficiency implications on national programs which are current challenges and underscores the need for a dedicated study to identify optimal review coverage rates.


**3.	Proposed next steps**

•	Develop a standardised reporting template for EM Reviewers 
Design and pilot a structured reporting template to be used by EM reviewers during the review process. This template should: 
o	Capture the level of agreement or discrepancy between EM analyst and reviewer data entries. 
o	Record which data fields were reviewed (e.g. species code, length, fate, disposition). 
o	Note any specific issues or recurring errors (e.g. misidentification of similar species, inconsistent application of fate codes). 
o	Allow reviewers to flag uncertainties or anomalies for further investigation or feedback to analysts. 
o	Be compatible with existing EM data systems to support integration, storage, and analysis. 

•	Investigate optimal reviewer coverage rates for catch verification
o	 Conduct analysis to underpin reviewer coverage rate for catch verification for target species, bycatch and SSI’s
 
 
•	Expanding participation and technical collaboration 
o	To ensure the relevance and applicability of these protocols across the region, we suggest that this work be expanded to include input from other national EM programmes as well as from EM technical service providers. Their operational experience, software-specific considerations, and field-level insights are critical for refining the protocols and ensuring they can be effectively adopted across diverse EM system configurations.  
o	A centralised repository for feedback on the fields. Given the highly technical nature of this work and its collaborative necessity, it is also proposed that the development and versioning of these protocols be transitioned to the proposed DCC GitHub repository. This platform will support transparent cross-collaboration, facilitate contribution tracking, and enable iterative refinement of the protocols in alignment with emerging technologies and field practices. 



